  "body": "Slice 2 of #93: a MILLING tool's own shape - cutter, neck and shank - drawn as a spun half-outline in the tool editor, the same way slice 1 (#475) did the holder. Lathe tools are untouched and keep their own editor (owner's ask on #93).\r\n\r\n## What changed\r\n- **Law (`camcraft-tools::cutter_profile`)**: `check(tool, points)` validates a drawn cutter against the tool's numbers and refuses by name: every spun-profile refusal (empty / too few points, negative or non-finite radius, open ends, runs back, ...), a turning tool, an outline that does not start at the tip, is not exactly the stated diameter within the flute length (never wider, never short), does not end at flute + shank length, is wider than the stated shank above the flute top, or folds back over itself. `parametric(tool)` is the outline the numbers draw. Slack 0.001 mm.\r\n- **Stored data**: a new component `schema::ToolCutter` (reader/writer `app::tool_cutter`). It is **display only**: no generator, simulation or judging law reads it, so the numbers stay the truth. A drawn cutter moves no operation stamp (pinned).\r\n- **Command**: `SetToolCutter::new(world, name, Some(points) | None)`, validated at creation, one undo step (undo restores the previous outline). `ChangeTool` refuses a number edit that would break a drawn cutter, by name (\"reset the cutter profile to Parametric first\"). `DeleteTool` / `DeleteEntity` snapshot it, so undo restores it.\r\n- **MCP**: `get_tool_cutter_profile` and `set_tool_cutter_profile` (`crates/app/src/mcp/cutter_tools.rs`). Both go through the same `SetToolCutter`, so `undo`/`redo` work. Refusals use the law's own wording.\r\n- **Project file**: additive `cutter` key on the tool record (`[z, r, arc]` per point). **No project-version step**: when the key is absent you get the outline the numbers draw, which is what every older file means. Save-load-save is byte-identical. A file whose drawn cutter disagrees with its tool is refused by name.\r\n- **UI**: the Profile tab gains **Draw holder / Draw cutter**. The holder view stays the default and is unchanged. Draw cutter has Parametric (follows the numbers) / Custom (point table with arcs, insert/remove), the shared flat side view (the drawn cutter is filled when it validates and outlined in red when not, with the holder above it), a live refusal by name, a summary line (cutter Ø, flutes, overall), and **Apply cutter**, which queues one command. Parametric + Apply clears back to the numbers' outline. The holder view's side view now draws the landed drawn cutter. `side_view` and the points grid are shared between both views.\r\n- Decisions: `docs/decisions/93-spun-cutter-profile.md`.\r\n\r\n## How tested\r\nGated **on this branch after #848 merged** (origin/main 2046357b merged in; the earlier local gate-tmp workaround was dropped and deleted, never pushed).\r\n- `camcraft-tools` unit tests `cutter_profile::tests::*` (4): every catalog milling tool passes its own law; turning tools refused; refusals by name; a number edit re-judged.\r\n- `crates/app/tests/tool_cutter_profile.rs` (5): `a_drawn_cutter_lands_undoes_and_moves_no_stamp`, `a_cutter_that_disagrees_with_the_numbers_is_refused_by_name`, `a_number_edit_that_breaks_the_drawn_cutter_is_refused_and_delete_restores_it`, `the_drawn_cutter_round_trips_and_older_files_load_without_one`, `the_cutter_is_drawn_and_undone_over_mcp`.\r\n- `camcraft-ui` `egui_app::tests::tool_cutter` (4, AccessKit labels/state): the cutter view sits beside the holder, Parametric follows the numbers, Custom keeps edits, Apply cutter is one command and undo/redo walk it, a cutter that disagrees with the numbers is refused live and at Apply. Slice 1's `egui_app::tests::tool_profile` and `tool_holder` re-run green.\r\n- Quick gate `cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/main` on head 943eee69: tools, 135 app suites (incl. tool_cutter_profile, tool_spun_profile, tool_body, holder_library, project_roundtrip, mcp*), 11 ui suites: all ok (14.5 min). `cargo clippy -p camcraft-tools -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-ui --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\r\n- A slice-1 UI test (`a_profile_refusal_retires_when_the_outline_validates`) first failed when the Draw holder / Draw cutter row sat above the holder view: the extra row pushed Apply profile out of view. The toggle now sits on the Body/Profile tab row, so the holder view keeps its height. The test was not changed.\r\n\r\n## Not exercised\r\n- The on-screen look of the side view was not hand-checked (no GUI launched). No snapshot PNG changed.\r\n- Release tier and `--ignored` lanes were not run.\r\n\r\n## Follow-ups\r\n- #852 (P1, effort:high, area:sim): the simulation and the reach laws should read a drawn neck where it is narrower than the shank cylinder. Not wired here on purpose, and nothing in `tool_body::judged` / `stack` or any collision law is touched.\r\n- Not mine, but noted: slice 1's drawn **holder** profile still has no MCP tool (#799 closed via #825, which shipped no MCP file).\r\n\r\n## Still owed on #93\r\n- Segment/parametric editing per stock tool kind beyond \"numbers draw it\" (for example editing the ball or chamfer by its parameters inside the outline editor).\r\n- The issue's \"preview snapshot\" test (asserted structurally here instead, per the snapshot law).\r\n- Feeding the profiles into the simulation and laws (#852; the issue lists it as out of scope).\r\n\r\nChangelog: Tool editor: draw a milling tool's own cutter, neck and shank as a spun outline (Profile tab, Draw cutter), validated against its stated diameter and lengths, undoable, saved with the project, and settable over MCP (set_tool_cutter_profile / get_tool_cutter_profile) (#93)\r\n\r\nEffort: high (not escalated)\r\n\r\nRefs #93\r\n\r\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\r\n",
